{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target conflates reproducibility with the prevention of displacement. Recomputing a deterministic workspace after reclamation can regenerate identical evidence, but it does not prevent the original completed evidence from being silently displaced when the workspace is reclaimed; detectability after the fact is not a 'guarantee never to silently displace.' Moreover, because the problem already stipulates a deterministic workspace, a certificate of no hidden non-deterministic inputs is either circular or insufficient, as it records a property already given rather than the actual workspace state needed to certify whether evidence was completed before the crash.","target":"215e98de9c706cd6fed5907f781e5954bf35c9b310292fcbda3f6e24bf552385"},{"attack":true,"case":"The protocol mandates deterministic replay from the last barrier to reconstitute the workspace before deciding whether evidence was completed, but this replay step is a destructive write operation on the workspace itself. If the crashed process had in fact completed the evidence but merely failed to write the terminal record, the replay overwrites that completed evidence, silently displacing it before the protocol ever evaluates completion. A protocol guaranteed never to silently displace evidence must not perform destructive reconstruction on a workspace whose contents are unknown; it must first preserve or non-destructively inspect the existing state.","target":"a5dc1a1879aeadc3845b931b39ae716e7cd3bacd8cc56a5108c7f7e14558d350"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target replaces the required absolute guarantee with a conditional BFT guarantee that depends on extraneous assumptions—independent storage nodes and an honest supermajority—which are not stated in the problem and are not guaranteed to hold. BFT consensus only ensures safety if fewer than one-third of witnesses are faulty, so the protocol is not 'guaranteed never' to displace evidence; it is merely guaranteed not to under specific external conditions. Furthermore, the target entirely ignores the given determinism of the workspace, which is the central property that should enable safe reclamation, making the protocol over-engineered and substantively misaligned with the problem.","target":"b471f5ed8f776d29b1c8719822c829f293c742350d639a443484306cc2872700"}]}